    "text": "A total score is calculated for questions 1-13 and reported as a raw score and the percentage of items endorsed (raw score / number of items multiplied by 100), to provide an indication of the proportion of symptoms the respondent identified with.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "MDQ Subscale Scores",
    "text": "Subscale scores are also reported using 10 of the 13 symptom items:\nPositive Activation (items 3, 4, 8, 9): assesses increased energy/activity, grandiosity, and decreased need for sleep. Individuals endorsing symptoms defining Positive Activation are not likely to report significant levels of negative affect and are likely to be energetic and extraverted. Individuals scoring high on Positive Activation may be less likely to rate their symptoms as impairing given that increased levels of energy and activity may be experienced as advantageous to some degree, especially if they are mild in nature. This factor is strongly associated with a bipolar disorder diagnosis.\nNegative Activation (items 1, 2, 6, 7, 12, 13): assesses irritability, racing thoughts, levels of negative affectivity, and distractibility. This factor is strongly associated with bipolar disorder as well as a range of other disorders, many of them (e.g. depressive disorders, PDs, PTSD, GAD, substance use disorders) characterised by emotion dysregulation and/or transdiagnostic personality traits such as neuroticism and disinhibition. Clients high in Negative Activation may be at risk for engaging in impulsive behaviour in emotional situations.\nThree items (5, 10, and 11) loaded highly onto both Positive and Negative Activation factors and were removed from the subscale model.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "MDQ Clinical Percentile Information",
    "text": "Clinical percentiles are presented for the subscales based on data from Carpenter et al. (2020) collected from N = 1,787 clinical outpatients presenting for treatment across a range of diagnoses. A percentile of 50 means the client has scored at the average level compared with the clinical group for that score.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "MDQ Normative Data",
    "text": "Clinical percentiles are presented for the two subscales as developed by Carpenter et al. (2020) on over 1,700 outpatients presenting for treatment across a range of diagnoses. A percentile of 50 means that the client has scored at the average level compared with the clinical group for that subscale.\nProfessional Access\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "reportScoringGuidance": [
   {
    "documentSha256": "22f17b292323d1623ffd3fc582d8ba730d70dee428dc8fe9deebbb34ad33a31f",
    "pageNumber": 2,
    "text": "A total score is calculated for questions 1-13 and reported as a raw score and the percentage of\nitems endorsed (raw score / number of items multiplied by 100), to provide an indication of the\nproportion of symptoms the respondent identified with.\nMDQ Screening Criteria\nIn order to meet the threshold for positive screening regarding bipolar disorder, the scoring\nmethod is as follows:\n- A score of 7 or more for questions 1-13 (53% of items endorsed)\nAND\n- \"Yes\" to item 14 asking if the symptoms clustered in the same time period\nAND\n- Symptoms caused either \"Moderate\" or \"Serious\" problems (item 15).\nMDQ Subscale Scores\nSubscale scores are also reported using 10 of the 13 symptom items:\n- Positive Activation (items 3, 4, 8, 9): assesses increased energy/activity, grandiosity, and\ndecreased need for sleep. Individuals endorsing symptoms defining Positive Activation are not\nlikely to report significant levels of negative affect and are likely to be energetic and extraverted.\nIndividuals scoring high on Positive Activation may be less likely to rate their symptoms as\nimpairing given that increased levels of energy and activity may be experienced as\nadvantageous to some degree, especially if they are mild in nature. This factor is strongly\nassociated with a bipolar disorder diagnosis.\n- Negative Activation (items 1, 2, 6, 7, 12, 13): assesses irritability, racing thoughts, levels of\nnegative affectivity, and distractibility. This factor is strongly associated with bipolar disorder as\nwell as a range of other disorders, many of them (e.g. depressive disorders, PDs, PTSD, GAD,\nsubstance use disorders) characterised by emotion dysregulation and/or transdiagnostic\npersonality traits such as neuroticism and disinhibition. Clients high in Negative Activation may\nbe at risk for engaging in impulsive behaviour in emotional situations.\nThree items (5, 10, and 11) loaded highly onto both Positive and Negative Activation factors and\nwere removed from the subscale model.\nMDQ Graphing\nOn first administration, two graphs are presented: a total score bar graph plotting the raw score,\nand a subscale bar graph showing the percentage of items endorsed. On multiple",
    "usage": "source_guidance_for_review_not_executable_scoring",
    "runtimeEnabled": false
   }
  ],
